 Based on the project documentation and current codebase review, here's the current state of the virtual art gallery
   project and unimplemented features:

   Implemented Features:

       * User role system (admin, curator, staff, tour guide, auction participant) with dedicated dashboards
       * Authentication and authorization
       * Artwork management (CRUD operations)
       * AI-powered curation and recommendations
       * Virtual tours and collections
       * Auction system with bidding
       * Review and feedback system
       * S3 integration for image storage (beget.com)
       * Advanced image upload (drag-drop, archives, links) with AI analysis
       * Image processing (thumbnails, watermarks, NFT generation)
       * 3D gallery visualization with Three.js and WebXR support
       * Real-time communication via Socket.IO
       * IoT device framework (routes exist, but full integration pending)

   Unimplemented Features/Next Steps:

       1. IoT Sensor Integration - Full implementation of Raspberry Pi/Arduino sensor data collection and feedback
   processing
       2. VR Device Integration - Beyond WebXR, native support for VR headsets
       3. AR Functions - Augmented reality features for mobile devices
       4. Multi-User Mode - Real-time collaborative gallery experiences
       5. Blockchain Integration - Full NFT marketplace with blockchain verification
       6. AI Algorithm Improvements - Advanced ML models for better recommendations and personalization
       7. Additional Room Types - Themed galleries and exhibition spaces
       8. Performance Optimization - 3D rendering improvements, caching, and SQL query optimization
       9. Testing Suite - Unit, integration, and E2E tests
       10. Security Enhancements - Advanced monitoring, logging, and penetration testing

   Architectural Notes:

       * The codebase is primarily TypeScript-consistent (contrary to some outdated documentation)
       * All imported components and routes exist
       * The project has a solid foundation with most core features operational
       * Focus should shift to advanced features and production readiness

   The project is approximately 80-90% complete for MVP, with remaining work focused on advanced integrations and
   optimizations.
